=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The BIND `named` DNS daemon on `dns_server` (10.200.0.2).

**Mechanism:** Both campus hosts (`host_1_1_1_1` and `host_2_1_1_1`) correctly point their resolver at `10.200.0.2`. When they send UDP/TCP queries to port 53, there is no process listening — `named` has crashed — so every query silently fails, returning no addresses for `web0.local` through `web3.local`. With no IP resolved, all subsequent HTTP requests also fail (curl code 000). The failure is universal across both hosts and all four `.local` names because it is entirely server-side.

**Localization:** `dns_server` — `named.service` is loaded and set to *enabled* in systemd, but its runtime state is `failed`. No `named`/`bind9`/`dnsmasq` process exists (`pgrep` empty); `ss` shows nothing bound to port 53; nftables contains only Docker-internal loopback NAT rules that are irrelevant to inbound DNS queries.

**Routing claim debunked:** Phase 1 (get_reachability) confirms `host_1_1_1_1` reaches `10.200.0.3` (same /24 as `dns_server`) with 0% loss. Phase B (ospf_snapshot) shows all six FRR routers with Full adjacencies and 10 OSPF routes each. The path to `10.200.0.2` is intact; the DNS daemon is simply not running.

**Root cause:** The `named` daemon on `dns_server` is in a systemd `failed` state — it either crashed after lab startup or failed to start — leaving port 53 with no listener.

**Fix:** Run `systemctl start named` (or `systemctl restart named`) on `dns_server` to bring the BIND daemon back up. No routing or OSPF changes are needed.
=== END ===